#### The question is
Why the TF Buffer doesn't get updated for so long (~3 sec) in the node subscribing to an image topic (Terminal 4) during the waiting period (meaning the main thread is actually doing nothing)? Shouldn't TfListener and the main node executor be working in different threads? Here is the relevant tflistener constructor, which should use its own spinning thread by default:
```cpp
tf_buffer_ = std::make_shared<tf2_ros::Buffer>(this->get_clock());
tf_listener_ = std::make_shared<tf2_ros::TransformListener>(*tf_buffer_);
```
and how we wait in the image callback:
```cpp
void callbackTopic(const sensor_msgs::msg::Image::ConstSharedPtr image) {
	try {
		geometry_msgs::msg::TransformStamped tmp;
		tmp = tf_buffer_->lookupTransform("odom", "base_link", image->header.stamp, rclcpp::Duration::from_seconds(0.5));
	}
	catch(tf2::TransformException & ex) {
			RCLCPP_ERROR(get_logger(), "%s (timeout=%.3fs)", ex.what(), 0.5);
	}
}
```
What could be causing that deadlock? It seems the image callback is blocking the tflistener thread somehow.

This example may seem an extreme case (processing FullHD images at 30 Hz with computer at 100% CPU usage), but it is the easiest way to reproduce the problem with a minimal setup. For a real-world example, I can see this issue easily using VSLAM/nav2 packages on a simulated robot (gazebo) running all on the same computer with under 70% CPU usage. The VSLAM nodes subscribing to image topics will often deadlock like this during 1-2 secs. I don't remember seeing this kind of issue on ROS1 Noetic but I will try to redo the same example on ROS1 to compare. 

#### Expected behavior
Terminal 4 should not show any errors like in Terminal 3, even on high load.

#### Actual behavior
Terminal 4 shows error about not receiving a TF in the timeout time (500 ms), while it should have received it during that long period.
